{
  "coreName": "DuckStation",
  "systemId": "PS1",
  "systemNames": {
    "PS1": "PlayStation"
  },
  "author": "Connor McLaughlin (stenzek) and the DuckStation contributors",
  "url": "https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    192,
    16,
    2048
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A PlayStation is small - 2MB of RAM, 1MB of VRAM, a 512KB ROM - and what fills memory is the picture: at 8x the hardware renderer's VRAM is 8192x4096 pixels, 128MB, and when a real graphics card draws it that much again is kept so a state can carry it (mmap) and once more as the buffer the card writes into (invisible, in no state). The numbers are room, not use: a savestate holds the pages that were touched. waterbox/run-wbx.c carries the same five and must be kept in step.",
  "video": {
    "_comment": "The frame is the console's own raster: as wide and as tall as the video mode makes it, from 256x224 to 640x480 on an NTSC console and to 576 lines on a PAL one, with the picture where the game put it - times the Internal Resolution when the hardware renderer draws it. The live size is reported per frame; 6144x4608 is room for the widest mode with no overscan cropped, at 8x. It is shown 4:3, as a television showed every one of those modes.",
    "width": 6144,
    "height": 4608,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 53693175,
    "vsyncDenominator": 897619,
    "_comment_vsync": "An NTSC console's field rate: the video crystal over 3413 ticks a line and 263 lines, 59.826Hz. A PAL console is 53203425 over 3406 by 314, 49.761Hz. The core reports the one the machine booted with (GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator), which the disc's region decides when the Region setting is auto.",
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The hardware renderer notices when its GL calls land on a context that is not the one its objects came from (the bridge's context id, which moves on every load), lets go of everything, builds a device again and uploads the console's video memory - which the core fetched from the card before the state was taken. So a state this core makes on a real GPU opens again in a later session.",
    "drawEveryFrame": true,
    "_comment_drawEveryFrame": "Never tell this core to stop drawing; turbo skips only the readback. What the hardware renderer draws stays in video memory on the card and the next frame is drawn over it, so a frame not drawn is a picture lost, not work deferred."
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "The SPU puts out 44100Hz stereo, 737 frames of it per NTSC field and 886 per PAL one. Nothing is stretched or resampled on the way: a frame's samples are the ones the chip produced.",
    "rate": 44100,
    "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".cue": "PS1",
    ".chd": "PS1",
    ".pbp": "PS1",
    ".ecm": "PS1",
    ".exe": "PS1",
    ".psexe": "PS1",
    ".ps-exe": "PS1"
  },
  "_extensions_note": "What opens as a PlayStation game when dropped on the frontend. .bin, .iso and .img are left out on purpose: they are a dozen machines' disc images, and ares - which answers for those machines - is asked first. A project can still name one in its Disc slot.",
  "input": {
    "name": "PlayStation Controllers",
    "_comment": "Two ports, and in each the DualShock's seventeen buttons and its two sticks; then the console's own controls. A digital pad is the first fourteen of a port; what a port actually holds is the Controller setting, and the core says which of these wires are live. A stick axis is the byte the pad reports - 0 at the left or the top, 255 at the right or the bottom, 128 at rest - so a movie can hold any value a game could ever read. Reset is the button on the case. Previous Disc and Next Disc move a selector over the project's discs and touch nothing; Disc Swap is the lid - up for as long as it is held, and closing on the selected disc (or, with the selector untouched, on the next one).",
    "buttons": [
      "P1 Up",
      "P1 Down",
      "P1 Left",
      "P1 Right",
      "P1 Start",
      "P1 Select",
      "P1 Square",
      "P1 Cross",
      "P1 Circle",
      "P1 Triangle",
      "P1 L1",
